  "summary": "Distinctive Ottoman ceramic ware featuring intricate floral and geometric designs in cobalt blue, turquoise, and coral red on a white background.",
  "description": "Characterized by vibrant colors and detailed patterns inspired by both Islamic art and Chinese porcelain, these ceramics were highly prized in Ottoman courts and beyond, reflecting the empire's cultural sophistication and trade connections.",
  "culturalContext": "Iznik ceramics — Ottoman Anatolia's court pottery (15th-17th c.) — reached the Islamic world's glaze perfection: cobalt-and-turquoise underglaze, the \"Armenian bole\" tomato red (c. 1555), saz leaves, tulips, carnations and calligraphic cartouches on mosque tiles (Süleymaniye, Rüstem Pasha) and court dishes. The mosque-tile panels made Istanbul's interiors garden-mirrors; the industry's decline after 1620s left technical puzzles modern craft revivalists (Funda Bali etc.) still decode.",
